Z||000000||olympic||11-25-1999||01:07 PM||||Hey, does anyone know the name or possibly the websites of companys who will ship a complete car? Also, what is involved in importing a car from the US into Canada? <BR>BTW, I've found an 88 coupe near Salt Lake City. The guy says that the body and interior are good but the timing gear on the cam is stripped. The car has a factory rear wing and the dual exaust cut out rear facia. It sounds like he might be the origonal owner!! Asking price is $500.  If anyone is interested I'll post his email address. P.S- I am so tempted to just rent a dolly and drive the 1100 miles to get it but pulling a car across the mountains doesn't sound like fun.....||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sage||11-25-1999||08:20 PM||||Just a thought, but you might try checking with some of the trucking firms close to you. See if anybody does runs to Salt Lake. A flat bed or lowboy would obviously be most suited, but a box would work.  Truckers hate to "dry run" back home, so alot of them look for something to haul back after a delivery,if they don't allready have something lined up.  Like I said, just a thought.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000002||Monkeyman||11-26-1999||04:02 AM||||I drove a truck for 15 years (got smart a couple of months ago and quit).  Flatbedders haul cars all the time, but they would only use it as a partial load.  (Are you gonna pay them $1.25/mile to have exclusive use of their trailer?)  You can't put one in a standard dry box, though.  There's no way to tie one down.  Your best bet would be to have it hauled on a "portable parking lot" (car transport).  They can haul a car cheaper than about anybody else because they can fit up to 11 cars at a time.  $500 cross country is about average.  Delivery into Canada is no problem, either.  They do it all the time.  I don't have any idea about registration or title transfer, though.  Look in your local yellow pages under "Brokers".  I'll dig out my old contacts and try to get back to you with some specific companies.  All I have handy are peoples names.  I'll try to get a hold of them this weekend (most drivers aren't home during the week). ||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||11-26-1999||05:13 AM||||Go to a good book/magazine store and buy a<BR>copy of Hemmings Motor News.  Its about the<BR>size of telophone book.  Most all of the transport companies have placed ads there.<BR>Many of the ads will have web sites. When I bought my Fiero back in May, I contacted about a dozen transport companies. Most of them would not ship to North Dakota, we are not on their east/west route. The rates that<BR>were quoted to me ran from $800-$1200 for open carier from Michigan to North Dakota. I finally got frustrated and Called U-haul. I reserved a car transport trailor in Michigan.<BR>Jumped in my truck, drove to Michigan and trailored my NOS baby home. It was alot of driving, but very much worth it.  I have had friends that have had very good luck with transport compainies.  Good Luck.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||11-26-1999||10:23 AM||||Seems like I was always renting or borrowing a flatbed trailer to haul vehicles. Finally, a couple years ago, I asked a buddy for about the third time if he would loan me his trailer. He just looked at me and exclaimed, "Go buy your own D@MN trailer!" So I did! I now have a nice 18 foot, tandum axle, electric brakes, open flatbed trailer with dovetail and stowaway 6 foot ramps. It's great having my own rig and I've used it plenty. I go fetch broke down vehicles (I rack up about 30k-40k miles a year). I haul stuff to and from auto swap meets. You can also haul old junk to the landfill or big lumber home from the store for that new workshop you are building.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 11-26-1999).]||||reg||1
